/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Plot cylinder pressure p against crank angle θ, measured from TDC, with angles before TDC (bTDC) negative.
   - Ignition lag is a chemical and physical delay: radicals must build up and the kernel must grow before burning becomes self-sustaining.
   - Once the kernel is about a few millimetres across, a flame front grows out of it.
   - The burnt gas is three to four times less dense than the unburnt charge at the same pressure.
   - Laboratory engines describe the burn by the mass fraction burned xb, from 0 at the spark to 1 at the end.
   - Ignition lag is roughly a fixed time, in milliseconds, set by chemistry.
   - To keep peak pressure near 15° aTDC, the spark must therefore be advanced as speed rises.
   - Read the three stages as a checklist when an engine runs badly.
*/
