/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - So a 40 mm flame path burnt in 1.4 ms gives a flame travel velocity of 0.040 ÷ 0.0014 ≈ 29 m/s, even though the front burns into the charge at only a few m/s.
   - ṁb = ρu Af Sb
   - ṁb is the mass burning rate (kg/s); ρu is the density of the unburnt charge (kg/m³); Af is the flame front area (m²); Sb is the burning velocity of the front relative to the unburnt gas (m/s).
   - The flame front area starts near zero at the plug, grows as the kernel becomes a large front, and shrinks again when the front reaches the piston, the head and the far wall.
   - This is why chamber shape and plug position matter.
   - The rate of pressure rise is the slope of the p–θ curve, dp/dθ, in bar per degree of crank angle (bar/°CA).
   - Two things follow.
   - The figure compares three burn lengths in one engine.
*/
