/* book exam traps: Laminar burning velocity is below 1 m/s; quoting 20 m/s for SL is wrong. Tens of m/s is the flame travel velocity seen from the cylinder head. | High flame speed alone does not fix dp/dθ: the front area, the unburnt density and the cylinder volume at that moment all enter. | bar/°CA, bar/ms and bar/s are different units: 2.05 bar/°CA is 36.9 bar/ms at 3000 rev/min but 24.6 bar/ms at 2000 rev/min. | The slope between two points is an average; the steepest slope lies inside the interval and is higher. | A faster burn gives more work only up to a point: past it the engine becomes rough and the end gas, though it has less time, is squeezed harder. */
